### Plan-quality review
**Verdict:** FLAWED
**Reviewed:** CALP-10 — writes the picked-up ticket id into a per-session marker file at pickup, and has the Stop hook read that marker to bill the turn's tokens to that ticket.
**Defects:**
- Asserts the cause ("no binding at pickup") and jumps straight to a fix, with no diagnosis anywhere in `### Problem & goal` or `### Design` of *why* cost currently lands in `__unassigned__` — the PO asked for cost to stop pooling; the repo records that the dominant cause of exactly this weeks-long pooling was `usage-log.sh` resolving the ticket-id prefix to a hardcoded `TIX`, so an explicitly-named `ADT-54` never matched and the marker stayed empty (`defaults/hooks/usage-log.sh:28-35`, ADR-001) — **solves an adjacent problem (class 1)**, and violates working-style #1 (mechanism before fix) — expected: the plan names the line producing the wrong value, then the binding step if the binding is genuinely the gap.
- Sub-steps 1a/1b build a per-session marker writer and a Stop-hook reader of that marker as if neither exists; both already ship — `usage-log.sh` writes `.adt-state/current-tix.d/<session>` and `adt-token-log.sh` reads it as its **primary** attribution signal (`defaults/hooks/adt-token-log.sh:21-23`, TIX-188) — **re-implements what exists (class 10)** — expected: the plan states that the marker path already exists, that the gap is only its *trigger* (a prompt must literally name `PREFIX-NNN`, which conversational pickup never does), and scopes the work to the new trigger plus the broken prefix resolution.
**Checked and found sound:** (a) **single-writer / concurrency (class 6)** — keying on the session id is the right call and the plan says why, so two parallel sessions genuinely cannot cross-bill; this is the shape the repo itself settled on after the flat last-writer-wins marker (TIX-233). (b) **complexity nothing forces (class 2)** — a marker file plus a hook read is about as small as this gets; no simpler alternative is being passed over. (c) **agent-writable state (class 4)** — the marker is agent-written, but this is cost accounting, not a gate on the agent's own work, so writable state is not a defect here.
**Notes:** I deliberately did **not** score the unverified `$CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` assumption as a plan defect — the repo's own recorded position is that runtime-environment assumptions are build-time MUST-verify risks, not plan blockers, and that framing is explicitly kept; the plan not pinning it in `### Risks` is a disclosure nit, not a design flaw. Forward-only attribution (already-pooled rows stay pooled, later needing `adt-reattribute.sh`) is a scope question for the PO, not a defect I can call against the stated problem.
